WEBVTT

00:00:00.135 --> 00:00:02.767
This example shows an unnormalized appointment record.

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:06.912
The row contains patient information, provider information, appointment time,

00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:11.012
a comma-separated list of service codes, and a matching list of charges.

00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:13.452
That design creates several problems.

00:00:13.452 --> 00:00:18.017
A foreign key cannot easily validate each service code inside a comma-separated list.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.202
Joining to one service is difficult.

00:00:20.202 --> 00:00:23.127
The charges can become misaligned with the service codes.

00:00:23.127 --> 00:00:25.957
The row also mixes several different subjects.

00:00:25.957 --> 00:00:31.152
Before normalizing, state the problem clearly: one row is trying to represent an appointment and several

00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:33.742
service occurrences at the same time.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:39.057
The first normalization step will separate those repeated service values into individual rows.

00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:42.432
Normalization is easier to understand as anomaly prevention.

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:46.907
Repeating facts in many rows makes updates inconsistent, inserts awkward, and deletes risky.

00:00:46.907 --> 00:00:51.757
Splitting data into well-defined entities reduces that repetition.

00:00:51.757 --> 00:00:56.960
The goal is not to create the maximum number of tables; it is to store each fact in a defensible place.
